  <section id="risks">
    <p class="eyebrow">Plan Health-Check</p>
    <h2>Risks worth keeping visible</h2>
    <ul class="hc-list">
      <li class="fixed"><strong>The bias is retired.</strong> The Portugal/Italy preference no longer touches the ranking. The data says <b>Portugal</b> first at 40, Madeira + Mallorca second at 37, Portugal + Sicily third at 35, and Iceland fourth at 35; genuine warm water only shows up at Turkey's sea and Hawaii.</li>
      <li class="solid"><strong>Hybrids resolve the core tension.</strong> All five hybrids keep a Portugal opener and hand the swimming to warm water elsewhere. Madeira openers buy epic hiking at the cost of extra flight legs and FNC wind risk; mainland openers stay calm and cheap. Crete and Sicily legs feed the Italy/Greece bias but press the preferred maximum; the newest hybrid, Madeira + Mallorca, is the only one that keeps the Madeira hiking week and stays within-preference.</li>
      <li class="head"><strong>Madeira runs on gates and buffers.</strong> Every Madeira variant needs verified ticket protection or deliberately buffered separate tickets, the Lisbon buffer night before the onward hop, and a hard family-airfare gate. The Sicily variants also rely on a thin Lisbon&ndash;Palermo market whose exact 2027 operating dates remain unconfirmed.</li>
      <li class="act"><strong>Italy drifted expensive.</strong> The Salento-Maratea-Amalfi file now reads ~$11.4k-16.3k realistic, with a preference-fit trim around ~$12.4k-14.8k. It is an excluded stretch reference, not a budget-safe pick.</li>
      <li class="act"><strong>Hawaii's own rollup crosses the preferred maximum.</strong> Hawaii's line-item budget table sums to ~$11k&ndash;15.8k with insurance. Combined with only nine hotel nights, a been-there Maui factor, and moderate dry-season fire exposure, it sits #15 before complete quotes land.</li>
      <li class="act"><strong>Maui + Kauai is the premium scenery benchmark.</strong> It fixes Hawaii's length penalty with 12 nights and adds Nā Pali, Waimea and Hanalei, but Maui is a repeat, Hāʻena/boat days are fragile, and the reconciled $14.6k&ndash;21.6k band places it #21 despite the same 35/55 raw score as the shorter Hawaii route.</li>
      <li class="head"><strong>Turkey is a warm-water wild card, not the automatic winner.</strong> The Turkish Riviera has the warmest non-Hawaii sea on the board, but June heat, moderate fire exposure, long-haul airfare, the scoped advisory, and a reconciled high case near $17.4k keep it an excluded reference at 36/55.</li>
      <li class="fixed"><strong>Croatia is excluded outright.</strong> Croatia scores 39/55 at the best warm-water price, but the family did Croatia in 2026 and this route repeats Split/Hvar/Korčula. Excluded from the recommendation by explicit decision; the row stays on the scoreboard for reference.</li>
      <li class="act"><strong>The Pittsburgh commitment drives the date math.</strong> Returning on Jun 23 is valid and preferred; every plan must keep the family in Pittsburgh for the full days Jun 24-26. Some plans still use Jun 9-22 for schedule or price reasons, while Balkans and California move into early July.</li>
      <li class="head"><strong>Complexity still matters.</strong> Sardinia/Corsica, Sicily/Malta, Greece via Lisbon, and the Balkans all offer major variety, but each adds either ferry, cross-border, flight-chain, or four-base friction. Balkans also picked up July peak-pricing risk.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

